{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":1299827,"num_turns":96,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"50154218-c807-4248-9df3-28a55e648446","total_cost_usd":7.834045499999998,"usage":{"input_tokens":1026582,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":92999,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":1082709,"outputTokens":94511,"cacheReadInputTokens":115451,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":7.834045499999998,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Done. **7/7 workloads pass**, at 4.5–5.2x the reference on small shapes and 9.2–9.8x on large ones.\n\n## What the kernel does\n\nA single Triton kernel, one program per row, holding the whole 2048-wide row in registers. The add, the sum-of-squares reduction, and the scale all happen in one pass, so each input element is read once and each output written once — 3×2 bytes/element, the memory-traffic lower bound. All arithmetic is float32, rounded to bfloat16 exactly once at the store, matching the reference's single trailing `.to(dtype)`.\n\nThree optimizations, each measured:\n\n1. **Streaming cache modifiers** (~9% on large shapes). Row data is single-use, so loads are `.cg` and stores `.cs`. `weight` is deliberately left at default caching — it's the one input every program reads and should stay resident.\n2. **Pre-resolved launch path** (15.3µs → 6.1µs on small shapes). Five of seven workloads have 1–79 rows, where the kernel is ~2µs and Triton's per-call argument binding costs more than the work. Compile once via `warmup()`, then call the resulting launcher directly — the same launcher the normal path reaches, with the same arguments.\n3. **Dropping `@torch.no_grad()`** (~1.5µs/call). A raw Triton launch tracks no autograd op; I confirmed outputs come back with `requires_grad=False` and no `grad_fn`, including under `inference_mode` and with grad-requiring inputs.\n\n## Two measurement traps worth flagging\n\n**My first `_hooks_active()` check was wrong and silently cost 2x.** The hook slots hold `HookChain` objects that are *never* `None`, so `is not None` always returned True and the fast path never ran — it looked like a working optimization while quietly always taking the fallback. The correct test is whether `.calls` is empty. This is the kind of bug that shows up only as \"slower than expected,\" so I bisected the dispatch path rather than trusting the design.\n\n**My local benchmark initially disagreed with the harness.** Timing a single hot buffer flattered large shapes by ~10% and made the cache-modifier change look like a *regression* (28.9→33.0µs). Re-benchmarking with rotating cold buffers matched the harness, which then showed the change nearly doubling large-shape speedup. I re-tuned `num_warps`/`waves_per_eu` on cold buffers with interleaved A/B rounds to cancel thermal drift.\n\n## Correctness\n\nBeyond the 7 workloads I ran 39 edge cases: batch sizes from 1 to 32768 including threshold neighbours, non-contiguous and transposed inputs, deliberately misaligned pointers (exercising the JIT fallback), adversarial values (exact cancellation to zero, all-zeros, 1e4 and 1e-4 magnitudes, mixed-scale rows), zero weight, empty batch, input-mutation and determinism checks, and a cold-cache clean-tree run in a fresh process. Worst observed error was 0.76 of the tolerance budget at 100% element match.\n\nThe kernel is not bit-identical to the reference on large shapes — about 0.0008% of elements differ, from float32 reduction ordering. I verified this is benign rather than assuming it: the differing elements sit at ~0.17 of the allowed tolerance.\n\nOne judgement call worth surfacing: the device-index cache is guarded against the caller switching GPUs (it re-resolves if `out.device.index` disagrees), and I read the hook state through `knobs` on every call rather than caching it, since a profiler can replace the slot rather than append to it — a stale reference would silently keep taking the fast path. Dev scripts are removed; only `kernel.py` and `solution.json` remain.","ttft_ms":3238,"ttft_stream_ms":3237,"time_to_request_ms":87,"type":"result","duration_ms":1416776,"uuid":"24494936-b213-4c7a-bf23-fe19c6360107"}
